VERDICT: FAIL -- the DUT never warned the user about the factory default password.  The
`manager` account still authenticates with the factory password `friend`, and the warning
line `% Default password needs to be changed.` appeared 0 times in 6 logins.
      console login, swi_d ......... 2 logins, no warning
      telnet login, swi_d .......... 1 login, no warning
      SSH login, swi_d ............. 1 login, no warning
      console login, swi_b ......... 2 logins, no warning (a different model, a build
                                     3 weeks older, also still on `friend`)
      the DUT's log ................ no `Default password for user 'manager' needs to be
                                     changed.` line anywhere
      instrument check ............. a `banner exec` marker DID appear on all 3 paths, in
                                     exactly the position the warning is specified for
      HTTP cross-check ............. RestCONF answered 200 OK with 11848 B, not the 403
                                     that the factory-default-password state specifies

FAIL CONDITION:
  WHICH CHECK FAILED.  The DUT must print the line `% Default password needs to be
  changed.` at every login as `manager` while that account holds the factory password
  `friend`.  It printed that line 0 times in 6 logins.

  AGAINST WHICH EXPECTED RESULT.  TFS 2616 spec 2616.1 requires the warning at every
  `manager` login when the password is `friend`.  The TFS worked example places the line
  after the version banner and immediately before the first prompt.  TPS row 271 marks
  this feature Supported: YES on the x960 at Priority 1.  The TFS lists no new command,
  no licence and no excluded platform.  So nothing had to be configured to make the
  warning appear.

  THE PRECONDITION HELD.  `manager` / `friend` authenticated on swi_d (STEP 1) and on
  swi_b (STEP 6).  The factory password is still in place on both units.

  WHY THIS IS A PRODUCT FAILURE AND NOT A METHOD FAILURE.  Four reasons, each measured.
  1. The instrument was proved able to see a message at that exact point.  A
     `banner exec CKPROBE-EXEC-BANNER-MARKER` line was configured, and the marker then
     appeared on the console, on telnet and on SSH, in the position the TFS example uses
     (STEP 7).  So the warning really was not sent.  The instrument did not miss it.
  2. Every access path the DUT offers was used: console, telnet and SSH.  The fourth path,
     HTTP, has no login to observe, because no Web GUI file is installed.
  3. A second x960 showed the same result.  swi_b is a different model on a build three
     weeks older, also still on `friend`, and it printed no warning either (STEP 6).
  4. A second subsystem agrees the DUT does not know its password is the factory one.
     The product's engineering page says an unchanged factory password puts the HTTP
     server in login-only mode, where most APIs return 403 with `API access requires
     admin password to be configured`.  RestCONF instead returned 200 OK with a normal
     11848-byte body (STEP 5c).  So this is not the CLI alone being quiet.  The internal
     check itself is not firing.

  THE LIKELY CAUSE, FROM THE PRODUCT'S OWN DOCUMENT.  The current "Forced Default Password
  Change" engineering page says, verbatim:
    "Currently, we only check that the password has been encrypted with an MD5 hash, as
     the default-generated config contains this but a user-generated config will not."
  The DUT stores the password as SHA-256, not MD5.  Measured:
    username manager privilege 15 password 8 $5$bJozIP4D$orvkW78S6QHN65L3EGGJpHS.QlHSjwuYbMynH5.Ifp2
  `$5$` is the crypt(3) prefix for SHA-256.  MD5 is `$1$`.  swi_b is the same: `$5$bJom.f4D$...`.
  A check that looks for an MD5 hash cannot match a SHA-256 hash, even when the password
  really is `friend`.  A developer needs to look at this.

TRIED:
  1. Console login as `manager`/`friend`, twice (STEP 2) ...... no warning.  Banner is
     `Last login:`, then the version line, then the prompt.
  2. Telnet login as `manager`/`friend` (STEP 3) .............. no warning.
  3. SSH login as `manager`/`friend` (STEP 3) ................. no warning.
  4. Search of the DUT log for any password line (STEP 3) ..... nothing.  ER361.1.02
     specifies a WARNING log entry when this check is active; there is none.
  5. Control login as `ckalt`/`testing123` (STEP 4) ........... no warning, as expected.
     The login path is otherwise identical, so a difference would have shown.
  6. HTTP / Web GUI login (STEP 5a, 5b) ....................... NOT MEASURABLE.  `show
     http` reports `GUI file in use : -`, so no GUI is installed.  Every URL tried
     returned HTTP 404 from nginx.
  7. RestCONF API call as `manager`/`friend` (STEP 5c) ........ HTTP 200 OK, 11848 B.
     Login-only mode would have given 403.  This is the cross-check that failed.
  8. Console login as `manager`/`friend` on swi_b, twice (STEP 6) .. no warning.
  9. `banner exec` marker on all three paths (STEP 7) ......... the marker APPEARED on
     all three.  The instruments are valid.
 10. Read the detection state from the CLI ................... impossible.
     `default-password ?` and `show default-password ?` both answer
     `% Unrecognized command`.

CAVEAT:  Three things this run did NOT establish.
         1. The `def_pwd_chg` boot variable was not read.  The older, gated feature (FDPC)
            is switched on by a U-Boot environment variable.  No CLI reports it, and
            reaching the bootloader needs a reboot.  No reload was done in this run,
            because the measurement path lives in the running configuration only.  So it
            is not proven that the warning is simply switched off on these units.  Against
            that reading: TFS 2616 is the later, ungated, warn-only feature, it names no
            gate, its excluded targets are None, and the TPS row for the x960 says Yes.
         2. The MD5 cause was not proved on hardware.  It rests on the engineering page
            plus the measured `$5$` hash.  The one experiment that would prove it is
            written out at the end of this log.  It was not run, because it touches the
            DUT's only administrative account on an unattended run.
         3. The manager password was never changed, so this run does not show what the
            DUT does after a password change.
