STEP 8 -- the mechanism, read from the product's own documents            done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  This step is analysis against the authority plus one measured value.  It is not a new
  measurement, and it is labelled as such.

  The "Forced Default Password Change" engineering page says, verbatim:
    "Currently, we only check that the password has been encrypted with an MD5 hash, as
     the default-generated config contains this but a user-generated config will not.
     This means that the most likely way to see the password warning is if the user has
     copied the value from the factory-generated config into a new config."

  The DUT's stored password, measured:
  username manager privilege 15 password 8 $5$bJozIP4D$orvkW78S6QHN65L3EGGJpHS.QlHSjwuYbMynH5.Ifp2

  `$5$` is the crypt(3) prefix for SHA-256.  MD5 is `$1$`.  swi_b is the same,
  `$5$bJom.f4D$KWOF...`.  On both units the manager password is stored as SHA-256, so a
  check that looks for an MD5 hash cannot match, even though the password is `friend` and
  authenticates as such.

  Three supporting facts, all measured:
  * `flash:/default.cfg` is the DUT's boot config, 1049 bytes, dated Oct 16 2026 06:22:58.
    It starts `! Startup-config saved on Wed Mar 22 17:15:11 2000 +0000 by manager via
    con0 (ttyS0)` and holds `service password-encryption` and the same `$5$bJozIP4D$...`
    line.  So it is a SAVED startup config, not a pristine factory file.  It is therefore
    NOT established from the bench whether the `$5$` form came from the factory or from a
    `copy running-config startup-config` done while the unit still had `friend`.  Either
    way the user sees the same thing, and it is not what the TPS row promises.
  * The salt lengths differ by origin.  Both units' `manager` hashes use an 8-character
    salt (`bJozIP4D`, `bJom.f4D`).  The password this case set through the CLI (`ckalt`)
    got a 16-character salt (`ifckkga8G0b4V0Gt`).  Two different generators, so the
    `manager` hashes were not made by an ordinary CLI `username ... password` command.
  * The state cannot be read from the CLI.  `default-password ?` and
    `show default-password ?` both answer `% Unrecognized command`.
  => STEP 8 done: the absence fits, end to end, a check that keys on the MD5 hash FORMAT
     rather than on the password VALUE, running on a build that stores SHA-256.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE ONE EXPERIMENT THAT WOULD CLOSE THIS, AND WHY IT WAS NOT RUN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  STEP 8's conclusion comes from the documents plus the measured hash format.  One
  experiment would measure it directly: set the manager password to an MD5-crypt (`$1$`)
  hash of `friend`, log in, and look for the warning.

    show running-config | include username manager      <- record the EXACT line first
    configure terminal
    username manager privilege 15 password 8 $1$<salt>$<md5crypt of 'friend'>
    end
    (then a console or telnet login as manager/friend, and look for the warning)
    restore:  username manager privilege 15 password 8 \
              $5$bJozIP4D$orvkW78S6QHN65L3EGGJpHS.QlHSjwuYbMynH5.Ifp2

  If the warning appears under `$1$` and not under `$5$`, the cause is proved on hardware.

  It was not run, for four measured reasons.
  1. It touches the DUT's only administrative account, on an unattended run.
  2. `security-password history 1` is configured.  It may refuse the restoring command,
     which would leave the unit on an MD5 hash of `friend` with no way back from here.
  3. If the device then decides it IS in the factory-default state, the older feature
     forces a password change instead of warning.  The login would demand a new password
     in a dialogue, and the console driver refuses such a dialogue by design.
  4. swi_a on this bench is already unusable because of a login lockout.
     A second lost unit would be a serious bench incident.
  It needs an attended session.
